  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure will dominate the region through \nWednesday morning. Afternoon dew points were lowered into the \nupper 30s to lower 40s given efficient boundary-layer mixing \nunder clear skies. For tonight, clear skies and light surface \nwinds will support rapid decoupling in the valleys after sunset.\nHowever, low-level wind profiles show flow just off the surface\nincreasing to 10 to 15KTs overnight. This will set up a \npronounced ridge-valley temperature split by Wednesday morning. \n\nDeep valley inversions will foster river valley fog development\nafter midnight. While fog will be widespread in lower river \nbasins, the increasing flow aloft should keep dense fog largely \nconfined to the valleys floors. \n\nPatchy frost is possible in isolated mountain cold spots away \nfrom rivers where fog fails to form (which should yield very \nlimited, if any, coverage). Given the limited spatial coverage \nand the late stage of the growing season in the highest terrain,\nwill forgo issuance of any frost headlines. Fog will burn off \naround 14Z Wednesday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nSurface high pressure shifts off the Mid-Atlantic coast on\nWednesday, establishing southwesterly return flow. Building\nupper-level heights will yield daytime high temperatures\nreaching the upper 70s to low 80s across the lowlands, which is\naround 10 degrees above seasonal normals. A northern stream \nshortwave trough traversing Quebec and the Great Lakes on \nThursday will push a weak cold front through the area Thursday \nmorning. Soundings reveal very shallow boundary-layer moisture \nwith dry air above H850, keeping PoPs near zero with no \nmeasurable rainfall expected. Low-level flow shifts to the \nnorthwest behind the front, knocking afternoon highs back into \nthe mid-70s to near 80 degrees for Friday, before temperatures \nquickly rebound into the low 80s on Saturday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAttention turns to the late weekend as Invest 92L in the\nsouthwestern Gulf evolves. High confidence exists in tropical\ncyclone development over the Gulf over the next couple of days\nas the system tracks north to northeast. As an upper-level\ntrough over the Central US interacts with this tropical system,\na deep plume of tropical moisture will lift northward into the\nTennessee and Ohio Valleys. Model guidance indicates\nprecipitable water values increasing significantly late Saturday\nnight into Sunday. While spatial and timing discrepancies remain\nregarding the exact surface low track and trough interaction,\nconfidence is increasing in widespread rainfall overspreading\nthe forecast area late Saturday night through Sunday. Ensembles\nsuggest general 24-hour rainfall totals of 1.0 to 1.5 inches\nacross the area. Details on QPF trends and potential excessive\nrainfall risks will be refined as the Gulf system organizes.",
